  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1229 PM MDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nA somewhat uncertain forecast for the rest of the day today. \nWhile a weak surface boundary - I'm not sure it's strong enough to\neven be called a cold front - is working its way south towards \nthe Denver metro. Where virga and light rain has been falling in \nNorth Park and south central Wyoming, temperatures are much \ncooler. Understandably, short-term forecast models don't seem to \nhave a great handle on both the initial wind shift we're seeing \nnow, and with the eventual stronger push of cooler air later this \nevening. In general, it seems the most likely scenario for the day\nis two rounds of convection - with the first round developing \nover the Front Range and generally remaining weak (given meager \nsurface moisture/MLCAPE), with chances of another round or two \nalong or behind the front later this evening. Initial \nshowers/storms this afternoon would be capable of producing gusty \nwinds as DCAPE increases to about 1200 J/kg. If the second round \nof convection does develop, the environment would favor multicell \nclusters or splitting supercells that would come with both a wind \nand hail risk. SPC's marginal risk with a CIG1 \"hatch\" for \nhail/wind across most of our plains seems to be a good \nrepresentation of today's conditional severe threat.\n\nThe flow aloft will turn a little more northwesterly on Wednesday\nas the upper ridge tries to establish itself closer to the Four \nCorners. Some moisture still lingers across the state, though \nguidance keeps the majority of convection across the far southern \nfringes of our CWA into south-central/southeastern Colorado during\nthe day. We should see at least isolated, weak convection across \nmost of the higher elevations, with precipitation chances dropping\noff towards the north and east.\n\nThe rest of the week will be dominated by a large ridge off to \nthe south, leading to a return of above normal temperatures and \ngenerally dry conditions. While temperatures will still be warm, \nthis round of heat looks much different than the previous spells \nof heat earlier in the month. First, 700mb temperatures are \nnowhere near as warm, with 700mb temperatures generally peaking \naround 16-19C... meaning highs should reach the mid 90s. There's \nalso still a decent amount of moisture trapped underneath this \nridge, with precipitable water values near or even slightly above \nnormal. We'll likely at least see a few showers over the high \ncountry each day, while the metro and plains stay dry.\n\nThe ridge is expected to weaken and slowly shift eastward by this\nweekend into early next week, allowing monsoon moisture to drift \nback into the forecast area. There's some modest timing \ndifferences in this period, but in general there's a good signal \nfor rain across the region by Sunday. Guidance has generally been \na day or two too quick to break down ridges here over the summer, \nso it's possible the better rain chances will wait until early \nnext week.",
